## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design lightweight auditing with low latency using Append Blobs and JWT parsing; demonstrates immutability, data-audit separation, and basic error handling.

## Key Concepts
- Append Blob usage for immutable, append-only logs
- JWT payload extraction from Authorization header
- Per-event audit entries with timestamp and eventId
- Resilient writes with basic retry/backoff

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Azure Function snippet (TypeScript)
import { ContainerClient, AppendBlobClient } from "@azure/storage-blob";
const containerClient = new ContainerClient("<storage-url>", new DefaultAzureCredential());
export async function run(context, req){
  const auth = req.headers["authorization"] ?? "";
  const token = auth.split(" ")[1] ?? "";
  const payload = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(token.split(".")[1], 'base64').toString());
  const userId = payload?.sub ?? "anonymous";
  const eventId = req.body?.eventId ?? crypto.randomUUID();
  const line = JSON.stringify({ ts: new Date().toISOString(), eventId, userId, status: 'received' }) + "\n";
  const audit = containerClient.getAppendBlobClient("audit.log");
  await audit.appendBlock(line, line.length);
  context.res = { status: 200, body: { eventId } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle audit log rotation or retention?
- How would you enforce per-tenant isolation for audits?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical Azure primitives for beginners: Functions HTTP trigger, Cosmos DB per-tenant partitioning, idempotency, TTL, Key Vault access via managed identity, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Azure Functions HTTP trigger
- Cosmos DB partitioning by tenantId
- Idempotency with idempotencyKey
- TTL configuration
- Managed Identity and Key Vault secrets
- Basic Application Insights telemetry

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal outline showing Key Vault and Cosmos DB usage
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require('@azure/identity');
const { SecretClient } = require('@azure/keyvault-secrets');
const { CosmosClient } = require('@azure/cosmos');

const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const kv = new SecretClient(`https://MY_VAULT.vault.azure.net`, credential);

async function getCosmosConn() {
  const secret = await kv.getSecret('CosmosDBConnectionString');
  return secret.value;
}

async function storeEvent(tenantId, evt) {
  const conn = await getCosmosConn();
  const client = new CosmosClient(conn);
  const container = client.database('Telemetry').container('Events');
  await container.items.create({ ...evt, tenantId });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement idempotency key storage and cleanup?
- How would you test TTL expiration and ensure data isolation per-tenant?

